   "When a rocket lifts off, the whole world seems to hold its breath. Millions watch the launch live; children press their faces to screens; for a few minutes, the quarrels of our planet seem very small. Yet every launch also raises an uncomfortable question. With so many problems on Earth – hunger, disease, climate change – can we really justify spending billions on space?",
   "Critics argue that we cannot. The budget of a single major space mission could fund thousands of schools or vaccinate millions of children. A crewed mission to Mars, some estimates suggest, could cost more than many countries spend on healthcare in a decade. ‘We should fix our own house before we build another,’ one campaigner told me. Others point out that the benefits of space technology tend to reach wealthy countries first, while poorer nations bear the heaviest costs of climate change and disease. It is a powerful argument, and it is hard to look at the numbers without some discomfort.",
   "But the picture is more complicated than it first appears. Space programmes have given us technologies we now take for granted. Satellite systems guide our aeroplanes, ships and phones; weather satellites warn of hurricanes days before they strike, saving countless lives. Research for space missions has contributed to advances in water purification, medical imaging and solar power. Much of the money spent ‘on space’ is in fact spent on Earth, employing scientists, engineers and manufacturers.",
