   "A new survey suggesting that fewer teenagers than ever have part-time jobs has sparked a lively debate among parents, teachers and employers.",
   "The survey, carried out by a youth employment charity, questioned 1,200 students aged 15 to 18 and found that just one in five had worked for money in the past year, compared with almost half of young people a generation ago. The charity’s director, Hannah Brooks, described the trend as ‘worrying’. ‘A Saturday job teaches things no classroom can,’ she said. ‘Turning up on time, dealing with customers, handling money, being part of a team. Employers tell us that young people who have worked are far more confident at interview.’ Last year the charity helped more than 3,000 young people into their first jobs.",
